steam-arm-setup (3.1) UNRELEASED; urgency=low

  * Upgrade to newest release from settings menu (Maintenance) or
    steam-arm-config upgrade: zip and .sha256 from GitHub over HTTPS,
    size, SHA-256 and GitHub digest checked, safe unpack, setup of new
    version run with --keep; --file ZIP for downloaded zip. Upgrade
    from zip never writes package files.
  * Package upgrade in one step: release lists
    steam-arm-setup_<version>_arm64.deb and .sha256; upgrade on package
    install downloads both, checks length, .sha256 line and GitHub
    digest, shows plan, takes setup lock, keeps copy of installed
    version, then runs
    apt-get install of checked file and steam-arm-setup --keep; restore
    offered on failure; --allow-older for older package
    (--allow-downgrades); --file takes package too. Release without
    package asset: package build steps.
  * Rollback protection: setup saves installed version to
    /var/lib/steam-arm/snapshots before each version change (installer
    and menu copies, /etc/steam-arm, owned files, settings backup,
    SHA-256 manifest); snapshot failure stops setup before any change.
    Maintenance, Go back to previous version, or upgrade --rollback.
    Unfinished change offered for restore, never restored on its own.
  * Automatic deletion of snapshots after successful upgrade or rollback:
    installed version plus 2 earlier versions kept (SNAPSHOT_KEEP 1 to
    5); damaged and half-written ones deleted.
  * Downgrade protection: setup over newer install stops before any
    change unless --allow-older.
  * Update / Repair runs setup script of installed version (older package
    no longer runs over newer install).
  * Optional check for new release at menu start, off by default.
  * Client channel shown by name (stable, Valve ARM channel of Steam Frame
    mode, Steam Beta Participation).
  * --remove deletes /var/lib/steam-arm and /var/cache/steam-arm.
  * Host facts CPU_LSE, CPU_CORES, PLATFORM (pi4, pi5, pi-other) and
    MESA_HOST in settings file; --detect prints them.
  * steam-arm --cpu-report: CPU and GPU profile of client window and
    games (two 60 s samples); Maintenance item and steam-arm-config
    cpu-report; hardware report appends static sections.
  * x86 client: FEX code cache off for client window processes, own
    keys merged into FEX app settings; reduced x87 precision for client
    program (x87-client, off by default); Multiblock off for client
    files after repeated client window restarts on Armv8.0 CPUs.
  * CPUs without Armv8.1 atomics: desktop interface and no friends view
    by default (client-light off restores Deck interface).
  * GPU forwarding notice on Raspberry Pi platforms when thunk libraries
    were not found; Information row GPU forwarding; run record reason.
  * Per-title keys tso=off and x87=reduced (Games, Emulation); automatic
    tuning on Raspberry Pi GPUs tries OpenGL version 3.3 and Zink first.
  * Experiment: client window through GPU for one start (lab-cef-gpu);
    CLIENT_GPU=on keeps ANGLE on Vulkan at every start until failure.
  * CPU report verdict section: client window state, restart loop, GPU
    process exits, top process group, FEX client settings, clock floor,
    Vulkan class, one next step. Information row Client window.
  * x86 client: FEX logging off for client window programs (SilentLog);
    site isolation off with lighter client window; CEF_HANG_KILLS off
    and CLIENT_TSO relaxed as opt-in settings (guarded); VK_HOST fact;
    setup block and first-start notice on CPUs without LSE atomics.
  * Per-title tso=off goes strict for one start after two failed runs;
    end hint on slow class; vk32-cpu reason; d3d=dxvk not applied on
    Raspberry Pi GPUs (five Vulkan features missing), WineD3D used.
  * Component pi-governor (off by default): governor performance while
    Steam runs on Raspberry Pi boards.

